WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:06.029
If you’ve been alive for the past ten years,
you’ve surely heard of two planes: the Airbus

00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:09.340
a380 and Boeing 787 Dreamliner.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:14.710
These two planes have dominated news cycles
worldwide because they’re both intensely

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:20.260
innovative—the a380 is the largest passenger
plane to have ever existed, while the Dreamliner

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:24.980
is one of the most efficient and has an unrivaled
focus on passenger comfort.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:30.720
Concealed by all the fanfare, however, is
a much deeper story on economics, innovation,

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.050
and how the airline industry works.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:39.780
So, believe it or not, work on the Airbus
a380 began back in 1988.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.600
More people were flying than ever, and airports
weren’t getting all that much bigger.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.200
Airbus eyed the success of the Boeing 747
and needed a bigger aircraft to compete.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:54.690
At first, Airbus considered making a super-wide
jet by placing two a340 fuselages side-by-side,

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:59.080
but later opted for the design we see today—a
fully double decker jet.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:05.650
An a380 can hypothetically carry as many as
868 people in an all-economy configuration,

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:12.140
although the densest in practice is 615 seats—this
is more than double that of the 787.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:17.390
Airbus decided to focus on making a high-capacity
aircraft because they believed in the hub-and-spoke

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.110
model of aviation.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.680
With the hub-and-spoke model, passengers traveling
from smaller airports—we’ll use Hartford,

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.310
Connecticut as our example—to a long-haul
destination, such as London, England, will

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:29.310
need to connect though a hub.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.280
In the case of Hartford, passengers would
likely take a short flight to New York, Atlanta,

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.250
or Chicago to catch their transatlantic flight
to London.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.820
Obviously this is inefficient for the passenger.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:43.060
On almost all routings between Hartford and
London, passengers have to fly away from their

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:46.000
destination in order to catch their transatlantic
flight.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.380
However, for airlines, there’s conceivably
an advantage.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:54.040
Let’s say, just for the sake of explanation,
that there are only six airports in the entire

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:59.840
United States—New York, Boston, DC, Los
Angeles, San Francisco, and Seattle.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.229
If an airline had one flight from every destination
to every other destination, they would need

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:07.840
to run 16 routes.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.379
If they just have one hub airport on each
coast, we’ll say New York and LA, they just

00:02:12.379 --> 00:02:16.900
need to have one route from each secondary
airport to each hub, and one transcontinental

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.050
hub-to-hub route—five in total.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:23.980
Obviously there will be quite high demand
on the one transcontinental route, so airlines

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:29.110
can put a large aircraft, such as the a380,
on that route to fulfill its demand.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:34.199
Since it’s release, the a380 has often been
placed on those long-haul, high demand routes—known

00:02:34.199 --> 00:02:35.239
as trunk routes.

00:02:35.239 --> 00:02:40.430
The second-busiest long-haul route in the
world—Dubai to London—sees eight a380’s

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.870
a day, and that’s in addition to five smaller
planes that fly that route.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.269
The Dreamliner serves a very different purpose.

00:02:47.269 --> 00:02:49.719
It’s a pretty modest size plane.

00:02:49.719 --> 00:02:54.150
It can only hold around 220 passengers in
a typical configuration.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:58.849
Its composite construction makes it extremely
light and fuel efficient, which helps reduce

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:00.549
operating costs.

00:03:00.549 --> 00:03:05.560
In the late 1990’s, Boeing started to see
slower sales on their large 747’s and 767’s,

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.349
and started to consider what to build next.

00:03:09.349 --> 00:03:12.891
They initially looked at creating a plane
called the Sonic Cruiser, which would have

00:03:12.891 --> 00:03:18.519
had the same fuel efficiency as conventional
aircraft, while flying 15% faster—just under

00:03:18.519 --> 00:03:20.079
the sound barrier.

00:03:20.079 --> 00:03:23.829
Airlines were initially enthused, however
after the attacks on September 11th and the

00:03:23.829 --> 00:03:27.560
rising cost of fuel, airlines were more interested
in fuel-efficiency rather than speed.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:34.819
The 787 delivered on that promise by reaching
up to 102 mpg per seat (2.41 L/100 km) compared

00:03:34.819 --> 00:03:38.999
to the a380’s paltry 74 mpg per seat (3.16
L/100 km.)

00:03:38.999 --> 00:03:46.529
The Dreamliner also has an absolutely enormous
range of up to 8,000 miles, and better yet,

00:03:46.529 --> 00:03:48.839
it’s efficient at that range.

00:03:48.839 --> 00:03:53.719
Boeing made such a relatively small aircraft
because they believe in an entirely different

00:03:53.719 --> 00:03:56.839
model of aviation—the point-to-point model.

00:03:56.839 --> 00:04:00.989
In this model, in order to get passengers
from Hartford to London, airlines just run

00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:03.739
a direct flight between Hartford and London.

00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:07.780
Obviously demand would be lower, but there
still is demand.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.359
In the past, to fly a route like this, an
airline would have had to use an aircraft

00:04:11.359 --> 00:04:17.030
with higher capacity than demand because smaller
airplanes couldn’t fly such a distance non-stop.

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:21.750
Given that, airlines resorted to the hub-and-spoke
model to concentrate all the demand on certain

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.470
routes where they could fill large, long-haul
planes.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:31.159
Now, with planes like the Dreamliner, airlines
can fly long routes with less demand while

00:04:31.159 --> 00:04:33.560
still being efficient.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.750
The hub-and-spoke model was also popular in
the past because airlines believed that it

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:41.330
was more cost-efficient to fly less flights
at higher capacity.

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:43.830
It’s simple economics really.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:48.530
Doing a lot of one thing together is cheaper
that doing a lot of one thing separately—it’s

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.229
economies of scale.

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:54.320
Except that doesn’t really extend to the
airline industry.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.270
For a larger aircraft you need more ground-staff,
more flight attendants, more check-in agents,

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.690
more fuel, more of pretty much everything.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:07.949
The only cost that stays the same is pilots,
and the only ones reduced are gates and take-off’s.

00:05:07.949 --> 00:05:12.069
When you’re now doing more flights at the
largest, most expensive airports, you end

00:05:12.069 --> 00:05:13.900
up spending more money.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:19.039
Airports like Hartford, Connecticut are cheap—labor
is cheap, take off fees are cheap, everything

00:05:19.039 --> 00:05:21.110
costs less than at JFK or Newark.

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:23.710
There are just fewer flights to compete with.

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:28.819
Not only that, but flying direct flights clearly
costs a ton less because airlines only have

00:05:28.819 --> 00:05:33.330
to pay the per flight costs that I talked
about a lot in my “Why Flying is so Expensive”

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:36.090
video once, rather than twice.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:41.449
United Airlines was a major innovator of the
point-to-point model with their Newark hub.

00:05:41.449 --> 00:05:46.069
Newark is an airport suited to serve smaller
planes, so United took the opportunity to

00:05:46.069 --> 00:05:51.250
open up direct flights to smaller destinations
on the British Isles using narrow-body planes

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.629
stretched to the upper limits of their range.

00:05:53.629 --> 00:05:59.389
United primary uses the Boeing 757 to reach
smaller destinations like Shannon, Ireland;

00:05:59.389 --> 00:06:04.750
Belfast, Northern Ireland; Glasgow and Edinburgh,
Scotland; and Manchester, Newcastle, and Birmingham,

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:06.439
England.

00:06:06.439 --> 00:06:11.219
Often the United Flight from Newark is the
only transatlantic flight serving these airports.

00:06:11.219 --> 00:06:16.120
United is able to operate these routes because
the East Coast of the US and British Isles

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.990
are just close enough together to reach with
a narrow-body plane.

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:25.960
With the 787, airlines are able to open even
longer routes between smaller destinations.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:30.789
Routes like these are called long-and-skinny—long
distance, but skinny demand.

00:06:30.789 --> 00:06:35.810
These includes routes like Tokyo to Seattle;
London to Chennai, India; Wuhan, China to

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:42.669
San Francisco; Beijing to Boston; Nairobi,
Kenya to Paris; Santiago, Chile to Madrid;

00:06:42.669 --> 00:06:47.979
Warsaw, Poland to Beijing; Doha to Edinburgh,
Scotland; the list goes on.

00:06:47.979 --> 00:06:53.539
The efficiency of this plane also has allowed
for an entirely new class of airline—budget

00:06:53.539 --> 00:06:55.039
long-haul carriers.

00:06:55.039 --> 00:06:59.819
The three main players in this category are
JetStar airlines based in Australia, Scoot

00:06:59.819 --> 00:07:05.599
airlines based in Singapore, and Norwegian
airlines based in both Scandinavia and London.

00:07:05.599 --> 00:07:09.779
Short-haul budget airlines have been possible
for a while because of efficient short-range

00:07:09.779 --> 00:07:14.009
airplanes, but this really is the first time
there has been such an efficient long-haul

00:07:14.009 --> 00:07:19.259
airplane, so these three airlines use the
reduction in operating cost to offer significantly

00:07:19.259 --> 00:07:23.849
lower ticket prices, while also using the
principles of budget airlines that I outlined

00:07:23.849 --> 00:07:26.430
in my “How Budget Airlines Work” video.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:32.300
What’s even more exciting for us consumers
is the upcoming Boeing 737 MAX.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:38.050
This plane is a redeveloped version of the
long-existing Boeing 737 featuring a larger

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:41.540
capacity, longer range, and higher fuel efficiency.

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:47.229
This means that we could conceivably see super
low-demand routes like Manchester to Cleveland,

00:07:47.229 --> 00:07:52.080
Lyon to New York, and Belfast to DC operated
in the near future.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.400
Norwegian airlines has already hinted at plans
to use to the 737 MAX to open up an auxiliary

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.060
transatlantic hub in Edinburgh—a relatively
small city.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:04.620
So, the a380 was a failure.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:09.009
Airbus hasn’t received a new order is years,
and it recently announced that it would be

00:08:09.009 --> 00:08:12.190
cutting back it’s production to only 12
a year.

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:18.309
Meanwhile the 787 has amassed almost 1,200
orders.

00:08:18.309 --> 00:08:22.430
Point-to-point flying has always been better
for the consumer, but with these recent innovations,

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:25.060
it’s now better for airlines too.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:31.120
Given that, it’s clear that point-to-point
flying is truly the future of aviation.

